---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const howToSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "HowTo",
  "name": "How to Password Protect an Excel File",
  "description": "Password protect an Excel file using Microsoft Excel built-in encryption or FileLocker.",
  "step": [
    { "@type": "HowToStep", "name": "Open your Excel file", "text": "Open the Excel workbook you want to protect in Microsoft Excel." },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", "name": "Go to File > Info > Protect Workbook", "text": "Click File, then Info, then Protect Workbook, then Encrypt with Password." },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", "name": "Enter a password", "text": "Enter a strong password and confirm it. Excel will encrypt the file using AES-256." },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", "name": "Save the file", "text": "Save the Excel file. The password protection is now applied." }
  ]
};

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How do I password protect an Excel file?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "In Microsoft Excel, go to File > Info > Protect Workbook > Encrypt with Password. Enter a password and save the file. Alternatively, use FileLocker to add an additional layer of encryption to the entire .xlsx file." }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the difference between Excel password protection and FileLocker?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Excel's built-in protection encrypts the file contents using AES-256. FileLocker encrypts the entire .xlsx file as a container, producing a .vault file. They serve different purposes: Excel protection integrates with the Excel format, while FileLocker works with any file type and requires the FileLocker unlock tool to open." }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can I recover an Excel file if I forget the password?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "No. Microsoft Excel uses AES-256 encryption which cannot be bypassed without the password. Similarly, FileLocker cannot recover lost passwords because it does not store them. Always keep your passwords safe." }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is my Excel file uploaded when I use FileLocker?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "No. FileLocker processes your Excel file entirely within your browser on your local device. Your file is never uploaded to a server." }
    }
  ]
};
---

<Layout
  title="How to Password Protect an Excel File | FileLocker"
  description="Learn how to password protect an Excel file using Microsoft Excel built-in encryption or FileLocker's browser-based tool. No uploads required."
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" slot="head" set:html={JSON.stringify(howToSchema)} />
  <script type="application/ld+json" slot="head" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />

  <div class="w-full bg-[#f8fafc] min-h-screen py-16 px-6">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">

      <nav class="flex text-sm text-gray-500 mb-8 font-medium">
        <a href="/guides" class="hover:text-brand-blue">Guides</a>
        <span class="mx-2">/</span>
        <span class="text-gray-900">Password Protect an Excel File</span>
      </nav>

      <div class="bg-white rounded-3xl p-8 md:p-12 shadow-sm border border-gray-100">

        <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-extrabold text-gray-900 mb-6 leading-tight">How to Password Protect an Excel File</h1>

        <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-600">
          <p class="lead text-xl text-gray-500 mb-8">
            Excel spreadsheets often contain sensitive financial data, personal information, or business records. Password protecting an Excel file helps ensure only authorized people can open or edit it.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Why Password Protect an Excel File?</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>Prevent unauthorized access to sensitive financial data</li>
            <li>Protect employee records, payroll sheets, or client information</li>
            <li>Secure spreadsheets before sharing over email or cloud storage</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4">Method 1: Password Protect an Excel File with Microsoft Excel</h2>
          <p>Microsoft Excel has built-in encryption that protects the entire workbook using AES-256. This is the most direct method if you have Microsoft Excel installed.</p>
          <ol>
            <li>Open the Excel file you want to protect.</li>
            <li>Click <strong>File</strong> in the top menu.</li>
            <li>Click <strong>Info</strong>, then <strong>Protect Workbook</strong>, then <strong>Encrypt with Password</strong>.</li>
            <li>Enter a strong password and click OK. You will be prompted to confirm the password.</li>
            <li>Save the file. The password is now required to open it.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>This method is well-supported. Anyone opening the file in Excel, LibreOffice Calc, or another compatible application will be prompted for the password.</p>

          <div class="bg-yellow-50 border border-yellow-100 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-2">Important: Sheet Protection vs. File Encryption</h3>
            <p class="m-0 text-sm text-gray-700">
              Excel also has a <strong>Protect Sheet</strong> feature that locks specific cells from editing. This is different from encrypting the workbook with a password. Protect Sheet prevents editing; Encrypt with Password prevents opening the file at all. Make sure to use <strong>Encrypt with Password</strong> if you want to restrict who can open the file.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4">Method 2: Protect the Excel File with FileLocker</h2>
          <p>FileLocker protects the entire <code>.xlsx</code> file as an encrypted container. This is useful when:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>You want to protect the file without relying on Excel-specific encryption</li>
            <li>You are sharing the file with someone who may not have Excel</li>
            <li>You want the file to be encrypted even before it is opened</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Here is how to protect an Excel file with FileLocker:</p>
          <ol>
            <li>Save and close your Excel file.</li>
            <li>Go to the <a href="/lock-file" class="text-brand-blue hover:underline">FileLocker File Protection tool</a>.</li>
            <li>Select your <code>.xlsx</code> file.</li>
            <li>Enter a strong password.</li>
            <li>Download the <code>.vault</code> file. To open it later, use the <a href="/unlock-file" class="text-brand-blue hover:underline">Unlock Tool</a> with the correct password, which will restore the original <code>.xlsx</code> file.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>Your file is processed entirely in your browser. Nothing is uploaded to a server.</p>

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4">What Is the Difference?</h2>
          <div class="overflow-x-auto">
            <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse mt-4">
              <thead>
                <tr class="bg-gray-100 text-gray-700">
                  <th class="text-left p-3 font-bold">Feature</th>
                  <th class="text-left p-3 font-bold">Excel Built-in</th>
                  <th class="text-left p-3 font-bold">FileLocker</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="border-t border-gray-100">
                  <td class="p-3">Encryption method</td>
                  <td class="p-3">AES-256 (inside .xlsx)</td>
                  <td class="p-3">AES-GCM (.vault container)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="border-t border-gray-100 bg-gray-50">
                  <td class="p-3">Requires Excel to open</td>
                  <td class="p-3">Yes</td>
                  <td class="p-3">No (FileLocker browser tool)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="border-t border-gray-100">
                  <td class="p-3">Works offline</td>
                  <td class="p-3">Yes</td>
                  <td class="p-3">Yes (after page loads)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="border-t border-gray-100 bg-gray-50">
                  <td class="p-3">File uploads to server</td>
                  <td class="p-3">No</td>
                  <td class="p-3">No</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4">Which Method Should You Use?</h2>
          <p>Use <strong>Excel's built-in encryption</strong> when your recipient will open the file in Excel and you want the password prompt to appear natively when the file is double-clicked.</p>
          <p>Use <strong>FileLocker</strong> when you want to add protection to any file type (not just Excel), when you prefer a browser-based tool with no installation, or when you want to share the encrypted file before the recipient even needs to open it in Excel.</p>

        </div>

        <div class="mt-12 space-y-4">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-4 mt-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-2xl border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 text-lg mb-2">How do I password protect an Excel file?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-600">In Microsoft Excel, go to File &gt; Info &gt; Protect Workbook &gt; Encrypt with Password. Enter a password and save. You can also use FileLocker to encrypt the entire <code>.xlsx</code> file in your browser.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-2xl border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 text-lg mb-2">What is the difference between Excel password protection and FileLocker?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-600">Excel's built-in protection encrypts the file within the Excel format. FileLocker encrypts the entire <code>.xlsx</code> file as a container, producing a <code>.vault</code> file that works with any file type.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-2xl border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 text-lg mb-2">Can I recover an Excel file if I forget the password?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-600">No. Both Excel's built-in AES-256 encryption and FileLocker use strong encryption that cannot be bypassed without the correct password. Always keep your passwords safe.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-2xl border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 text-lg mb-2">Is my Excel file uploaded when I use FileLocker?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-600">No. FileLocker processes your Excel file entirely within your browser. Your file is never uploaded to a server.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-12 text-center border-t border-gray-100 pt-10">
          <a href="/lock-file" class="bg-brand-blue hover:bg-brand-blue-dark text-white font-bold py-4 px-10 rounded-full shadow-lg transition-transform hover:scale-105 active:scale-95 inline-block text-lg">
            Password Protect Your Excel File
          </a>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
